  out of the world financial institutions  the chinese could compensate for that to  some degree they could they could take  more russian energy which they would  quite like to do if the russians can't  sell it to the west because of really  tough sanctions  and they certainly defend them just  about in the united nations and in other  world forums so with president xi  jinping effectively propping up russia's  economy by refusing to place sanctions  against them attentions turn to what's  in it for china  make no mistake china is waiting and  watching to see how this plays out the  war in ukraine could act as an example  for china if it decided to invade taiwan  beijing sees taiwan in a similar way to  how russia sees ukraine an unresolved  historical question whose future it is  theirs alone to decide  well there's no suggestion yet that the  country is planning an imminent invasion  we do know that taiwan is seen by china  as a breakaway province that will one  day reunite with the mainland  let me say this from the bottom of my  heart you have been an inspiration to  the tennis people in facing threat and  coercion from authoritarian power but  it's worth remembering that these two  countries are very different taiwan has  a much bigger economy than ukraine and  it's much further away geographically  than ukraine is from russia any invasion  would be much more difficult to execute  the thing about the ukraine crisis which  is so important is that the russians are  being pushed back not just by the  ukrainians but by world opinion  and world opinion is so um hostile to  russia and russia has become such a  pariah that in a way  it's made taiwan a bit safer because xi  jinping has realized and will realize  that the effect of this crisis  has been to strengthen the west that  said the world is now sitting up and  paying attention  the us has become one of the main  providers of arms and support to taiwan  and in response to what they perceive is  an increased thre